{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787123007,"nonce":"3AUVs9HJyeE8Tdz1ZAsBBERLU6I=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABPjXSqQ","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/114#issuecomment-5338682025"},"message":"**Correction: the diagnosis in this issue is wrong. The mesh is not the problem — the objective is.**\n\nI filed this claiming `relocate()` writes back a mesh that differs from the geometry its loop\nvalidated. That is false, and I should have checked it before filing. Measured on this issue's own\nAllen-Cahn problem and on a second (an L-shape driven by a compact source), the written-back mesh is\n**bit-identical** to the loop's last recorded state:\n\n    written-back mesh vs fem.adapt_history[-1][\"points\"]:  max |diff| = 0.000e+00\n\n**What is actually happening.** `objective=\"energy\"` descends the FE Dirichlet energy, and the\ndocstring asserted that \"on a steady problem the energy *is* the error norm\". That identity requires\n**no source term**. The Ritz functional is `J(v) = ½a(v,v) − (f,v)`, and it is `J` that satisfies\n`J_h − J_exact = ½‖u−u_h‖²_E`. With no load, `J = E`, so descending the energy descends the error —\nthe classic singular-mode L-shape, where it cuts the error 55% at fixed DOFs. Add a source (or a\nreaction term, as Allen-Cahn has) and `J_h = −E_h` at the discrete solution: minimising the error\nmeans **maximising** `E`, so descending it walks away from the solution.\n\nThe optimiser was never misbehaving. It did exactly what it was told — on the L-shape+source problem\nit cut `E` from 0.122521 to 0.107876 — while the true error rose 3.6×. Flattening elements is the\ncheapest way to lower `∫|∇u|²`, so the mesh degrades as a side effect:\n\n| | error | smallest angle |\n|---|---|---|\n| start | 5.72e-03 | 40.8° |\n| `objective=\"energy\"` | 2.04e-02 | **3.2°** |\n| default (equidistribution) | 9.15e-03 | 31.8° |\n\nNot inverted in either case, so a `det J \u003e 0` check passes at 3.2° — \"not tangled\" is far too weak a\nquality bar for this failure.\n\n**What still stands.** The `_op` restore bug that led me here was real and is fixed separately (a\nsecond `fem.solve()` after any `adapt=` run dispatched on the pre-adapt operator). And relocation\nstill does not *help* on either problem even with the correct objective: on the L-shape+source the\nerror rises monotonically from the first iteration at every step size tested, so the descent\ndirection is wrong there, not merely over-stepped.\n\n**Suggested scope for this issue**, retitled: make `objective=\"energy\"` either descend the true Ritz\nfunctional `½a(u,u) − (f,u)` (which reduces to the current behaviour when there is no load), or\nrefuse by name on a form carrying a source/reaction term. The docstring has been corrected to state\nthe validity condition, and the tutorial switched to the default objective plus a mesh-quality assert.","files":null},{"type":4,"timestamp":1791885629,"nonce":"PhpAF5krlv285a6BIu4sCcroYig=","metadata":{"github-id":"CE_lADORPa0Bs8AAAABNQwVU88AAAAHtjSNeg"},"status":2}]}